From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm: a733: dp: repair three error paths nothing has ever walked

Three defects in sun60i-dp.c, all of them on branches that run only after
something else has already failed. A test boot trains the link from
cold, follows the cable and puts a login prompt on /dev/fb0 with zero `err` and
zero `warn` lines from any of the four display modules -- and that is exactly
why these three survived: the success path is exercised on every boot, the
error paths are exercised by nothing.

⚠ SAID FIRST BECAUSE IT IS THE WEAKEST PART OF THIS PATCH: NONE OF THE THREE
PATHS HAS EVER EXECUTED ON HARDWARE, neither before this patch nor after it.
They were found by reading `drm-allwinner-a733-displayport-aux`..`drm-a733-dp-chain-a-hpd-bridge-on-the-typec-receptacle` applied in order onto a freshly unpacked
6.18.36 (items 1, 4 and 8 of a review of that code) -- not by a
board. Everything below is a derivation with file:line for each step, and each
fix carries a concrete falsifier at the end. Nothing here may be quoted as
hardware-proven.

Line numbers in `sun60i-dp.c` are the file as it stands AFTER `drm-a733-dp-chain-a-hpd-bridge-on-the-typec-receptacle`, i.e. the
tree this patch applies to. Line numbers in every other file are stock 6.18.36.

1. USE-AFTER-FREE OF THE drm_device THROUGH dp->aux.drm_dev
------------------------------------------------------------

.attach, sun60i-dp.c:3304-3309:

	dp->aux.drm_dev = bridge->dev;
	ret = drm_dp_aux_register(&dp->aux);
	if (ret) {
		dev_err(dp->dev, "DP AUX register: %d\n", ret);
		return ret;			/* aux.drm_dev stays set */
	}

The field is assigned before the call and not cleared when the call fails, and
from that return nothing clears it either:

  * drm_bridge_attach() calls .attach at drm_bridge.c:437-438 and, on a negative
    return, goes to err_reset_bridge (:440), which does
    `bridge->dev = NULL; bridge->encoder = NULL; list_del(&bridge->chain_node);`
    (:464-466). The bridge is now OUT of encoder->bridge_chain.
  * drm_encoder_cleanup() walks exactly that list to detach
    (drm_encoder.c:197-199), so .detach is never called for this bridge -- and
    sun60i_dp_aux_unregister_locked() (sun60i-dp.c:3261-3268, the only place
    that nulls dp->aux.drm_dev, at :3266) is only reached from .detach (:3354)
    and from .attach's OTHER failure branch (:3334). This branch reaches
    neither.
  * The error propagates sun60i_kms_output_init() (sun60i-kms.c:721-724) ->
    sun60i_kms_bind() (:941-943, `goto unbind_all`) -> `put_drm:` ->
    drm_dev_put() (:983). The drm_device is freed with dp->aux.drm_dev still
    pointing at it, and dp outlives it: dp is the DP platform device's driver
    data, not the KMS master's.

The dangling pointer is then dereferenced by the DP helpers on every DPCD
access: drm_dp_dpcd_read() and its write counterpart call drm_dp_dump_access()
(drm_dp_helper.c:666, :756, :788), which is `drm_dbg_dp(aux->drm_dev, ...)`
(:562-565). Every debugfs verb in this file that touches DPCD reaches it --
`train` through sun60i_dp_link_probe(), `dpcd` and `edid` directly -- as does a
hot-plug that arrives after the failed bind.

TRIGGER: drm_dp_aux_register() returns non-zero. Two ways inside it:
drm_dp_aux_register_devnode() (drm_dp_helper.c:2404) or i2c_add_adapter()
(:2408) -- an allocation failure, or a sysfs name collision.

WHAT CHANGES: `dp->aux.drm_dev = NULL;` in that failure branch, plus a comment
saying why the assignment cannot simply be moved after the call:
drm_dp_aux_register() opens with `WARN_ON_ONCE(!aux->drm_dev)`
(drm_dp_helper.c:2393), so the field must be set BEFORE it runs and can only be
undone afterwards.

NULL is a state this code already runs in and tolerates. .detach leaves it NULL
for the rest of the driver's life, and the log helpers take it: __drm_to_dev()
returns NULL for a NULL drm_device (drm_print.h:608-611) and
__drm_dev_vprintk() has an explicit `if (dev) ... else printk(...)` arm
(drm_print.c). So the fix trades a freed pointer for an unprefixed debug line,
not for a NULL dereference.

2. BOTH .attach FAILURE BRANCHES LEAVE THE DRIVER WITH NO DDC ADAPTER
----------------------------------------------------------------------

.attach opens by taking the I2C-over-AUX adapter away from this driver, because
drm_dp_aux_register() is about to add the very same struct i2c_adapter itself
(drm_dp_helper.c:2398-2408):

	if (dp->ddc_owner == SUN60I_DP_DDC_DRIVER) {
		i2c_del_adapter(&dp->aux.ddc);
		dp->ddc_owner = SUN60I_DP_DDC_NONE;
	}				  -- sun60i-dp.c:3299-3302

If drm_dp_aux_register() then fails (:3305), or if drm_bridge_attach() for the
Type-C receptacle's bridge fails (:3331), .attach returns an error having put
nothing back. Neither branch is left holding a registered adapter by accident:
drm_dp_aux_register() unwinds its own devnode when i2c_add_adapter() fails
(drm_dp_helper.c:2409-2412), and the second branch calls
sun60i_dp_aux_unregister_locked() (:3334), which removes what the core added.

So the driver returns with `dp->enabled` still true, an AUX channel that is up
and answering, `ddc_owner == SUN60I_DP_DDC_NONE`, and NO adapter registered
anywhere. EDID is gone and nothing says so. Worse, it does not heal:
sun60i_dp_aux_disable() only deletes the adapter it owns (:1372-1375), so with
ownership NONE an `echo 0 > aux_enable` removes nothing -- only the following
`echo 1` re-adds one, and only because ownership is NONE again by then.

TRIGGER: a user has brought AUX up by hand first -- `echo 1 > aux_enable`,
which is the pre-KMS path this file documents at :317-318 and :625-627, and
the only way ddc_owner is DRIVER at .attach time -- and the KMS master then binds and one of
the two calls above fails. The first is the same allocation/collision failure as
defect 1. The second is narrower on this board: drm_aux_hpd_bridge_attach()
refuses only a missing DRM_BRIDGE_ATTACH_NO_CONNECTOR (aux-hpd-bridge.c:164),
which our own .attach has already rejected, so what is left is
drm_bridge_attach()'s own -EBUSY/-EINVAL guards (drm_bridge.c:414-427) and the
-ENOMEM from its atomic_reset (:446-450).

WHAT CHANGES: the six statements that add the driver-owned adapter move out of
sun60i_dp_aux_enable() into sun60i_dp_ddc_add_locked(), unchanged, and both
failure branches call it back through a thin sun60i_dp_ddc_restore_locked()
that logs when even the re-add fails. One function rather than three copies is
the same reasoning sun60i_dp_aux_unregister_locked() already carries at the
other end of the hand-over.

The three i2c_adapter fields are re-filled on every add rather than kept from
probe, and that is required, not tidiness: i2c_del_adapter() ends with "Clear
the device structure in case this adapter is ever going to be added again" and
memsets adap->dev (i2c-core-base.c:1821-1823), and drm_dp_aux_register()
overwrites owner, dev.parent and name with its own (drm_dp_helper.c:2398-2402).
An adapter that has been through the core and come back is not the struct that
was set up the first time.

3. err_gates CLOSES GATES IT DOES NOT OWN
------------------------------------------

sun60i_dp_video_configure() opens both eDP_REG_PAD clock gates at :1006 and
jumps to err_gates at :1011 when sun60i_dp_video_transfer_unit() fails.
err_gates (:1028-1029) closes those two gates and returns -- and stops there.

On the .atomic_enable path that is wrong, because by then it is not the only
owner of the gates. sun60i_dp_train_output_enable(), added by `drm-a733-dp-enable-the-transmitter-output-before-training` (:2189-2202),
has ALREADY raised the same two gates (:2197) and set TX_OUTPUT_ENABLE and
INPUT_SOURCE_ENABLE (:2199-2200) -- and `drm-a733-dp-enable-the-transmitter-output-before-training` measured all four as required for
this board to reach clock recovery. err_gates therefore leaves the transmitter
driving a trained link with PAD_DP_LNK_CLK_CFG shut underneath it.
sun60i_dp_video_disable() takes all four back (:1189-1191); err_gates takes two.

TRIGGER, and this one needs no fault injection at all: any rung where training
succeeds and the transfer unit then does not fit. The lane ladder falls back to
one lane, sun60i_dp_train() returns 0, and sun60i_dp_video_transfer_unit()
returns -EINVAL on `sym16 > TU_SIZE * TU_FRAC_UNITS` because 1620 Mb/s over one
lane (LinkBW 162) cannot carry the built-in 1920x1200 mode (data rate 462).
A cable with one good lane produces exactly this.

WHAT CHANGES: the four writes become one function, sun60i_dp_video_output_off(),
called from both sun60i_dp_video_disable() and the error path, which is renamed
err_output because it is no longer only about gates. The register sequence
sun60i_dp_video_disable() emits is byte-for-byte identical to what it emitted
before; the error path now emits four writes instead of two, in the same reverse
order.

WHY THE SUCCESS PATH IS NOT TOUCHED -- AND IT IS THE ONLY PATH WITH A PROOF
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

The success path is the one thing here that silicon has signed off, so it is
the one thing this patch must not disturb. Statement by statement:

  * Defect 1 adds one store (`dp->aux.drm_dev = NULL`) inside a branch whose
    next statement is `return ret`.
  * Defect 2 moves six statements into a function called from the same place,
    under the same `if (dp->ddc_owner == SUN60I_DP_DDC_NONE)`, in the same
    order, with the same errno and the same dev_err string. The new calls sit
    inside `if (ret)` branches.
  * Defect 3's writes in sun60i_dp_video_disable() are the same three lines in
    the same order with the same values, emitted from a callee. `err_output` is
    a label reached only by `goto` from an error return.

No register write is added to, removed from or reordered on any path that
returns 0. No lock is taken, dropped or reordered: the two new helpers run under
dp->lock exactly where the code they replace ran, and the only structural change
to the locking is that .attach's second failure branch grows a braced
scoped_guard body where it had a single statement -- same scope, same mutex.

The one addition that is not on an error branch is `lockdep_assert_held(&dp->lock)`
in sun60i_dp_ddc_add_locked(). It compiles to nothing without
CONFIG_PROVE_LOCKING, which configs/a733_defconfig does not set, and the
assertion holds anyway: both callers of sun60i_dp_aux_enable() take the mutex
first (sun60i-dp.c:2418 before :2428, and :3106 before :3137).

VERIFIED
---------

  applies clean : yes -- `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` onto a freshly unpacked
                  6.18.36 source tree carrying every patches/*.patch
                  applied in glob order, i.e. `drm-allwinner-a733-displayport-aux`..`drm-a733-dp-chain-a-hpd-bridge-on-the-typec-receptacle` for this file. Dry run
                  and real apply both exit 0, no .rej, no .orig.
  builds        : yes -- `make ARCH=arm64 W=1 drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/` on that
                  tree with configs/a733_defconfig, native aarch64 gcc 14.2.0.
                  All four modules compile and the build log contains not one
                  occurrence of "warning" or "error". No new symbol is
                  referenced: lockdep_assert_held, i2c_add_adapter, strscpy,
                  dev_name, dev_err and writel are all already used in this
                  file.
  checkpatch    : `--strict --terse` reports 0 checks and 0 code warnings. The
                  two lines it does print -- one >75-char prose line and
                  "Missing Signed-off-by by nominal patch author ''" -- are
                  artefacts of this tree's headerless patch format and are
                  printed, one of each, identically for `drm-a733-dp-detect-reports-hot-plug-not-dpcd-valid` and `drm-a733-dp-chain-a-hpd-bridge-on-the-typec-receptacle`.
  structure     : braces, parentheses and brackets balance across the whole
                  file after the patch; the label set is unchanged except
                  err_gates -> err_output, and no label is duplicated; each of
                  the three new functions has exactly two callers, so nothing
                  is left unreferenced.
  on silicon    : NO, and see the warning at the top: neither the defects nor
                  the fixes have been on a board. The success path this patch
                  is careful not to touch has been; these three have not. A
                  clean W=1 build is not evidence that an error path is
                  correct -- it is evidence that it compiles.

FALSIFIERS -- one per fix, and what each would have to show
-------------------------------------------------------------

Defect 1 and defect 2 share a provocation, because they share a branch. Neither
can be reached by typing at a healthy board: drm_dp_aux_register() fails only on
an allocation failure or a name collision. Two honest ways in, in order of
preference:

  a) CONFIG_FAILSLAB=y + CONFIG_FAULT_INJECTION_DEBUG_FS=y, fail-slab armed
     across the KMS bind, so the kzalloc inside i2c_add_adapter() /
     device_register() is the one that fails. Nothing is edited.
  b) A one-line temporary substitution of `ret = -ENOMEM;` for the
     drm_dp_aux_register() call in .attach. Not committed, not built into
     anything that ships.

Run either with CONFIG_KASAN=y, and with AUX already up by hand so that
ddc_owner is DRIVER when .attach runs:

    echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/aux_enable
    ls -d /sys/class/i2c-adapter/i2c-*      # note the adapter that appears
    <provoke the bind>                      # expect: DP AUX register: -12
    ls -d /sys/class/i2c-adapter/i2c-*      # defect 2's observable
    echo 0 > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/train

  DEFECT 1, BEFORE: a KASAN "use-after-free read" report in drm_dp_dump_access()
  or __drm_dev_vprintk() on that last line -- or, without KASAN, a splat from
  whatever now occupies the freed drm_device.
  DEFECT 1, AFTER: no KASAN report; the drm_dbg_dp lines lose their device
  prefix, which is what a NULL drm_dev looks like.
  REFUTED BY: a use-after-free report AFTER the patch (the field is being set
  again from somewhere this reading did not find), or a NULL dereference in the
  log path (the tolerance claimed above from drm_print.h:608-611 is wrong).

  DEFECT 2, BEFORE: no i2c adapter in the second listing, and none after
  `echo 0 > aux_enable`; `cat .../link` still says the channel is up.
  DEFECT 2, AFTER: an adapter is present again. A DIFFERENT i2c-N is expected
  and correct -- it is a fresh IDR entry, not the old one.
  REFUTED BY: still no adapter after the patch; or an adapter that is present
  but whose transfers fail with -ENODEV, which would mean ddc_owner was not
  restored with it.

Defect 3 needs no injection, which makes it the strongest of the three:

    echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/aux_enable
    echo "1620 1"  > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/train
    echo configure > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/video

  The write must fail with -EINVAL and dmesg must carry the transfer-unit line
  ("... 1620 Mb/s over 1 lanes cannot carry 154000 kHz at 24 bpp"). If it does
  not fail, the premise is gone and nothing below means anything. Then read the
  two registers -- PAD_DP_LNK_CLK_CFG at 0x05760000 and
  DP_TRANSMITTER_OUTPUT_ENABLE at 0x05740080:

  BEFORE: 0x00000000 and 0x00000001 -- the transmitter driving with its link
  clock gate shut, which is the defect stated as two words.
  AFTER: 0x00000000 and 0x00000000.
  REFUTED BY: 0x00000001 / 0x00000001 after the patch (err_output did not run),
  or 0x00000000 / 0x00000001 after the patch (it ran and did not clear the
  transmitter).

  ⚠ If CONFIG_STRICT_DEVMEM refuses the /dev/mem reads, the same two values come
  from a temporary seq_printf of both registers placed ABOVE the
  `!dp->video_configured` early return in sun60i_dp_video_show() (:1269) --
  temporary, not committed, because the committed diagnostics rule is that
  nothing is added that the success path pays for.

OPEN
-----

1. DEFECT 3'S FIX IS A DECISION, NOT A DEDUCTION. The review offered two
   answers: clear all four, or leave the gates alone in the error path because
   ownership belongs to sun60i_dp_train_output_enable(). This patch takes the
   first, on the grounds that a defined-off state is easier to reason about than
   a state split across two owners -- but the second is defensible and no
   measurement separates them. If a future patch finds that a failed
   `configure` should leave a trained link intact for a retry, this is the line
   to revisit.

2. THE OTHER FIVE FINDINGS OF THE SAME REVIEW ARE NOT IN THIS PATCH.
   Review items 2, 3, 5, 6 and 7 -- the bpc
   latch across a sink change, the unlocked `dp->enabled` read in
   sun60i_dp_aux_transfer(), sun60i-de.c's err_clocks asserting a reset under a
   fetching layer, the discarded commit->arm() return, and the dev_err in the
   mode filter -- stand untouched and unrefuted. Item 3 in particular is a race
   this patch neither creates nor closes.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
@@ -541,6 +541,55 @@ static ssize_t sun60i_dp_aux_transfer(struct drm_dp_aux *aux,
 	return msg->size;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Add the I2C-over-AUX adapter under this driver's own ownership. Two callers:
+ * sun60i_dp_aux_enable() on the pre-KMS path, and the restore helper below,
+ * through which .attach's two failure branches put back the adapter they took
+ * off a user who had already opened AUX by hand. One function rather than
+ * three copies, the same reason sun60i_dp_aux_unregister_locked() is one at the
+ * other end of the same hand-over.
+ *
+ * The three fields are set on EVERY call, not once at probe: i2c_del_adapter()
+ * ends with "Clear the device structure in case this adapter is ever going to
+ * be added again" and memsets adap->dev (i2c-core-base.c:1821-1823), so an
+ * adapter that has been handed to the DRM core and taken back is not the struct
+ * that was filled in the first time.
+ */
+static int sun60i_dp_ddc_add_locked(struct sun60i_dp *dp)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	lockdep_assert_held(&dp->lock);
+
+	dp->aux.ddc.owner = THIS_MODULE;
+	dp->aux.ddc.dev.parent = dp->dev;
+	strscpy(dp->aux.ddc.name, dev_name(dp->dev), sizeof(dp->aux.ddc.name));
+
+	ret = i2c_add_adapter(&dp->aux.ddc);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(dp->dev, "I2C-over-AUX adapter: %d\n", ret);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	dp->ddc_owner = SUN60I_DP_DDC_DRIVER;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * The same, on a path that cannot report the failure to anybody: .attach is
+ * already returning an error of its own. Losing DDC here is not fatal -- AUX
+ * itself is untouched, and an aux_enable 0 -> 1 cycle rebuilds the adapter --
+ * but it is invisible without a line, and an invisible missing adapter reads
+ * as a dead sink.
+ */
+static void sun60i_dp_ddc_restore_locked(struct sun60i_dp *dp)
+{
+	if (sun60i_dp_ddc_add_locked(dp))
+		dev_err(dp->dev,
+			"DDC lost; aux_enable 0 -> 1 rebuilds the adapter\n");
+}
+
 /*
  * Bring the AUX channel up. Split out from probe() on purpose: it is what a
  * consumer calls once DisplayPort alt mode has actually been entered and the
@@ -612,18 +661,9 @@ static int sun60i_dp_aux_enable(struct sun60i_dp *dp)
 	 * conditional rather than unconditional. See enum sun60i_dp_ddc_owner.
 	 */
 	if (dp->ddc_owner == SUN60I_DP_DDC_NONE) {
-		dp->aux.ddc.owner = THIS_MODULE;
-		dp->aux.ddc.dev.parent = dp->dev;
-		strscpy(dp->aux.ddc.name, dev_name(dp->dev),
-			sizeof(dp->aux.ddc.name));
-
-		ret = i2c_add_adapter(&dp->aux.ddc);
-		if (ret) {
-			dev_err(dp->dev, "I2C-over-AUX adapter: %d\n", ret);
+		ret = sun60i_dp_ddc_add_locked(dp);
+		if (ret)
 			goto err_phy_off;
-		}
-
-		dp->ddc_owner = SUN60I_DP_DDC_DRIVER;
 	}
 
 	dp->enabled = true;
@@ -690,6 +730,25 @@ static void sun60i_dp_video_gates(struct sun60i_dp *dp, bool on)
 	writel(on ? VID0_CLK_GATING : 0, dp->pad + PAD_DP_VID_CLK_CFG);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Put the transmitter down together with the two gates underneath it, in the
+ * reverse of the order sun60i_dp_train_output_enable() raised them. All four
+ * writes or none: the gates and the two enables are one unit, and a state with
+ * TX_OUTPUT_ENABLE set over a shut PAD_DP_LNK_CLK_CFG is the one `drm-a733-dp-enable-the-transmitter-output-before-training` measured
+ * as not reaching clock recovery.
+ *
+ * Two callers, which is why it is a function: the ordinary teardown in
+ * sun60i_dp_video_disable(), and sun60i_dp_video_configure()'s error path.
+ * Both have already established that the block is reachable -- `enabled` for
+ * the bus clock and the reset, `pad` for the second register window.
+ */
+static void sun60i_dp_video_output_off(struct sun60i_dp *dp)
+{
+	writel(0, dp->base + DP_INPUT_SOURCE_ENABLE);
+	writel(0, dp->base + DP_TRANSMITTER_OUTPUT_ENABLE);
+	sun60i_dp_video_gates(dp, false);
+}
+
 static u32 sun60i_dp_video_bpc_code(u8 bpc)
 {
 	switch (bpc) {
@@ -994,7 +1053,7 @@ static int sun60i_dp_video_configure(struct sun60i_dp *dp)
 
 	ret = sun60i_dp_video_transfer_unit(dp, mode, bpp);
 	if (ret)
-		goto err_gates;
+		goto err_output;
 
 	/*
 	 * The transmitter output enable must wait until the link output is
@@ -1011,8 +1070,16 @@ static int sun60i_dp_video_configure(struct sun60i_dp *dp)
 
 	return 0;
 
-err_gates:
-	sun60i_dp_video_gates(dp, false);
+	/*
+	 * All four, not just the two this function opened. On the
+	 * .atomic_enable path sun60i_dp_train_output_enable() has already
+	 * raised the same two gates and set both enables, so closing the gates
+	 * alone would leave the transmitter driving a trained link whose clock
+	 * gate is shut -- the asymmetry sun60i_dp_video_disable() does not
+	 * have.
+	 */
+err_output:
+	sun60i_dp_video_output_off(dp);
 
 	return ret;
 }
@@ -1172,9 +1239,7 @@ static void sun60i_dp_video_disable(struct sun60i_dp *dp)
 	 */
 	writel(0, dp->base + DP_SRC0_STREAM_ENABLE);
 	writel(0, dp->base + DP_SRC0_ENABLE);
-	writel(0, dp->base + DP_INPUT_SOURCE_ENABLE);
-	writel(0, dp->base + DP_TRANSMITTER_OUTPUT_ENABLE);
-	sun60i_dp_video_gates(dp, false);
+	sun60i_dp_video_output_off(dp);
 
 	dp->video_on = false;
 	dp->video_configured = false;
@@ -2683,6 +2748,7 @@ static int sun60i_dp_bridge_attach(struct drm_bridge *bridge,
 				   enum drm_bridge_attach_flags flags)
 {
 	struct sun60i_dp *dp = bridge_to_dp(bridge);
+	bool restore_ddc = false;
 	int ret;
 
 	/*
@@ -2710,12 +2776,26 @@ static int sun60i_dp_bridge_attach(struct drm_bridge *bridge,
 		if (dp->ddc_owner == SUN60I_DP_DDC_DRIVER) {
 			i2c_del_adapter(&dp->aux.ddc);
 			dp->ddc_owner = SUN60I_DP_DDC_NONE;
+			restore_ddc = true;
 		}
 
+		/*
+		 * Assigned BEFORE the call and not after, because
+		 * drm_dp_aux_register() opens with WARN_ON_ONCE(!aux->drm_dev)
+		 * (drm_dp_helper.c:2393). So it has to be cleared again by hand
+		 * on the failure path: the core takes this bridge back out
+		 * of encoder->bridge_chain there (drm_bridge.c:463-466), so
+		 * .detach -- the only other place that nulls the field -- is
+		 * never called, and sun60i_kms_bind() unwinds to
+		 * drm_dev_put().
+		 */
 		dp->aux.drm_dev = bridge->dev;
 		ret = drm_dp_aux_register(&dp->aux);
 		if (ret) {
 			dev_err(dp->dev, "DP AUX register: %d\n", ret);
+			dp->aux.drm_dev = NULL;
+			if (restore_ddc)
+				sun60i_dp_ddc_restore_locked(dp);
 			return ret;
 		}
 
@@ -2741,8 +2821,11 @@ static int sun60i_dp_bridge_attach(struct drm_bridge *bridge,
 	 */
 	ret = drm_bridge_attach(encoder, dp->next_bridge, bridge, flags);
 	if (ret) {
-		scoped_guard(mutex, &dp->lock)
+		scoped_guard(mutex, &dp->lock) {
 			sun60i_dp_aux_unregister_locked(dp);
+			if (restore_ddc)
+				sun60i_dp_ddc_restore_locked(dp);
+		}
 		return ret;
 	}
 
